@attribute [Route(pageUrl)]
@layout DemosMainLayout

<PageMetaTags PageUrl="@pageUrl" Title="@metaTitle" Description="@metaDescription" ImageUrl="@imageUrl" />

<PageHero Title="@pageTitle">
    <SubTitleTemplate>
        @((MarkupString)pageDescription)
    </SubTitleTemplate>
</PageHero>

<DocsLink Href="@DemoRouteConstants.Docs_RadarChart" />

<Prerequisites PageUrl="@pageUrl" />

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="How it works" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="how-it-works">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Radar Chart</strong> component visualizes multivariate data with each variable represented on axes starting from the same point, making it ideal for comparing multiple groups across several categories.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
        <ol>
            <li>Add the <code>RadarChart</code> component to your page and set its dimensions (e.g., <code>Width="600"</code>).</li>
            <li>Prepare your chart data by specifying <code>Labels</code> for each axis and one or more <code>Datasets</code> with values and styling options.</li>
            <li>Configure chart options such as <code>Responsive</code> using the <code>RadarChartOptions</code> object.</li>
            <li>Initialize the chart in <code>OnAfterRenderAsync</code> by calling <code>radarChart.InitializeAsync(chartData, radarChartOptions)</code>.</li>
            <li>Use the demo's interactive buttons to randomize data, add datasets, or add new data points dynamically and see the chart update in real time.</li>
            <li>Refer to the demo code below for practical examples of data preparation, dynamic updates, and chart customization.</li>
        </ol>
        </div>
        This demo demonstrates how to set up a Radar Chart, bind your data, and interactively update the chart to fit your application's needs.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_01_Examples)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Click event" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="click-event">
    <Block>
        Set the <code>OnClick</code> callback to react when a radar data point is selected. The callback receives <code>ChartClickEventArgs</code> with the dataset, point index, label, and raw value; the demo displays the selected item below the chart.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_02_Click_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Export image" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="export-image">
    <Block>
        Export the chart as an image with <code>ToBase64ImageAsync</code>, which returns a base64 data URL you can show, store or send, or save it straight to a file in the browser with <code>DownloadImageAsync</code>.
        If the chart is still animating, both wait for the animation to finish, and the image is streamed from the browser, so Blazor Server needs no larger message size.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Keep a reference to the chart with <code>@@ref</code> and initialize it.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ToBase64ImageAsync()</code> for a PNG data URL, or pass <code>ChartImageType.Jpeg</code> and a quality from 0 to 1.</li>
                <li>Call <code>DownloadImageAsync("radar-chart.png")</code> to save the image as a file without sending it to .NET.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_03_Export_Image)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Show and hide datasets" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="show-and-hide-datasets">
    <Block>
        Show and hide whole datasets or single points from C#, check whether a dataset is visible and count the visible datasets.
        Visibility changed at runtime, from C# or a legend click, stays in place when <code>UpdateAsync</code> refreshes the data, because datasets are matched by <code>Key</code>, or else by label, and points by label.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Call <code>HideAsync(datasetIndex)</code> and <code>ShowAsync(datasetIndex)</code>, or <code>SetDatasetVisibilityAsync(datasetIndex, visible)</code>.</li>
                <li>Pass a data index too, for example <code>HideAsync(0, 2)</code>, to hide one point. This hides the point's marker only; the outline still passes through its value.</li>
                <li>Read the state with <code>IsDatasetVisibleAsync</code> and <code>GetVisibleDatasetCountAsync</code>.</li>
                <li>Refresh with <code>UpdateAsync(data, options, ChartUpdateMode.None)</code> to apply new data without animation; hidden datasets and points stay hidden.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_04_Show_Hide_Datasets)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Legend click event" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="legend-click-event">
    <Block>
        Set <code>OnLegendClick</code> to react when a legend item is clicked. The callback receives <code>ChartLegendClickEventArgs</code> with the dataset index, the legend text and whether the dataset is hidden after the click.
        Set <code>LegendClickToggles="false"</code> to stop the legend from hiding datasets, for example to open details instead.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add an <code>OnLegendClick</code> callback to the chart.</li>
                <li>Keep <code>LegendClickToggles</code> at <code>true</code> for the default hide and show, or set it to <code>false</code> to handle the click yourself.</li>
                <li>Use <code>DatasetIndex</code>, <code>Text</code> and <code>Hidden</code> from the event arguments.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_05_Legend_Click_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Hover event" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="hover-event">
    <Block>
        Set <code>OnHover</code> to react when the pointer moves onto a different point. The callback receives <code>ChartHoverEventArgs</code> with the same item details as <code>OnClick</code>.
        It is called only when the hovered point changes, and once with a <code>null</code> <code>DatasetIndex</code> when the pointer leaves all points, so it does not flood Blazor Server with calls.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add an <code>OnHover</code> callback to the chart.</li>
                <li>Check <code>DatasetIndex</code>: a value means a point is hovered; <code>null</code> means the pointer left all points.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_06_Hover_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Programmatic highlight" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="programmatic-highlight">
    <Block>
        Highlight points and show their tooltip from C# with <code>SetActiveElementsAsync</code>, for example to point at a value from a table next to the chart.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Pass one or more <code>ChartActiveElement(datasetIndex, index)</code> items to <code>SetActiveElementsAsync</code>.</li>
                <li>Pass an empty list to clear the highlight and the tooltip.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_07_Programmatic_Highlight)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Update modes and animation control" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="update-modes-and-animation-control">
    <Block>
        Choose how <code>UpdateAsync</code> animates with <code>ChartUpdateMode</code>, and control the running animation with <code>StopAsync</code>, <code>RenderAsync</code>, <code>ResetAsync</code> and <code>ClearAsync</code>.
        These map one-to-one to the Chart.js <code>update(mode)</code>, <code>stop()</code>, <code>render()</code>, <code>reset()</code> and <code>clear()</code> methods.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Call <code>UpdateAsync(data, options)</code> for the default animated update, or pass <code>ChartUpdateMode.None</code> to apply the change at once. An animated update moves each matched dataset from its current values to the new ones, and a new dataset grows in. Datasets are matched by <code>Key</code> when set, otherwise by <code>Label</code>; datasets that share a label, or have none, pair in order.</li>
                <li>Call <code>StopAsync()</code> to end any running animation; the chart stays as it was drawn at that moment. Call <code>UpdateAsync</code> again to animate to new values.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ResetAsync()</code> to put the chart back in its starting state, before its first animation, and <code>RenderAsync()</code> to draw that state. Stop any running animation first, or its next frame draws over the reset. Call <code>ResetAsync()</code> and then <code>UpdateAsync(data, options)</code> to regrow the chart from its starting state, as on first render.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ClearAsync()</code> to clear the canvas; <code>RenderAsync()</code> draws the chart again. A running animation draws over the cleared canvas on its next frame, so stop it first or wait for it to end.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_08_Update_Modes)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Radial scale" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="radial-scale">
    <Block>
        The radar chart draws its values on one radial axis, <code>Scales.R</code>, sent to Chart.js as <code>scales.r</code>. Chart.js starts a radar axis at the data minimum, but this library sets <code>BeginAtZero = true</code> by default, so the axis starts at zero unless you turn it off.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Scales = new RadialScales { R = new ChartRadialAxes { ... } }</code> on <code>RadarChartOptions</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>BeginAtZero = false</code> to start the axis from the data minimum, as Chart.js does, so values close together are easier to compare.</li>
                <li>Fix the range with <code>Min</code> and <code>Max</code>, and set the ring spacing with <code>Ticks = new ChartAxesTicks { StepSize = 10, ShowLabelBackdrop = false }</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Grid = new ChartAxesGrid { Circular = true }</code> for round rings instead of polygons, and <code>Reverse = true</code> to put the maximum at the center.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_09_Radial_Scale)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Angle lines and point labels" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="angle-lines-and-point-labels">
    <Block>
        Angle lines run from the center to each label, and point labels name each spoke of the radar. Chart.js shows both on radar charts by default.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.R.AngleLines = new ChartAxesAngleLines { ... }</code> with <code>Display</code>, <code>Color</code>, <code>LineWidth</code> and <code>BorderDash</code> (for example <code>[6, 4]</code>).</li>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.R.PointLabels = new ChartAxesPointLabels { ... }</code> with <code>Display</code>, <code>Color</code>, <code>Font = new ChartFont { Size = 14 }</code> and <code>Padding</code>.</li>
                <li>Give the labels a background with <code>BackdropColor</code> and round it with <code>BorderRadius</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Display = false</code> on either one to hide it.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_10_Angle_Lines_And_Point_Labels)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Start angle" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="start-angle">
    <Block>
        <code>Scales.R.StartAngle</code> rotates the whole radar, in degrees clockwise from the top. Chart.js uses 0, which puts the first label at the top.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Scales = new RadialScales { R = new ChartRadialAxes { StartAngle = 45 } }</code>.</li>
                <li>Use 0, 45 or 90 (or any angle) to choose where the first label sits.</li>
                <li>Leave it <code>null</code> to keep the Chart.js default of 0.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_11_Start_Angle)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Title and subtitle" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="title-and-subtitle">
    <Block>
        The title and subtitle plugins draw text around the chart area. Chart.js hides both by default; in this library the title and subtitle color defaults to <code>"black"</code>.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Title = new ChartPluginsTitle { Display = true, Text = "Team skills" }</code>.</li>
                <li>Add <code>Plugins.Subtitle = new() { Display = true, Text = "Season 2026" }</code>; it is drawn below the title.</li>
                <li>Move either one with <code>Position</code> (<code>"top"</code>, <code>"left"</code>, <code>"bottom"</code>, <code>"right"</code>) and align it with <code>Align</code> (<code>"start"</code>, <code>"center"</code>, <code>"end"</code>).</li>
                <li>Add space with <code>Padding = new ChartPadding { Top = 10, Bottom = 20 }</code>, and set <code>FullSize = false</code> to keep the title within the chart area width.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_12_Title_And_Subtitle)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Tooltip options" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="tooltip-options">
    <Block>
        Tooltip options decide which points a tooltip lists, where it opens and how it looks. Values left <code>null</code> use the chart's <code>Interaction</code> options and the Chart.js defaults.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Tooltip = new() { ... }</code> on <code>RadarChartOptions</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Mode = InteractionMode.Index</code> with <code>Intersect = false</code> to list every team on the hovered spoke, or <code>InteractionMode.Nearest</code> for the closest point only.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Position</code> to <code>"average"</code> (the middle of the listed points) or <code>"nearest"</code> (the point closest to the pointer).</li>
                <li>Style it with <code>Padding</code>, <code>CornerRadius</code>, <code>BorderColor</code>, <code>BorderWidth</code>, <code>BoxWidth</code>, <code>BoxHeight</code>, <code>BoxPadding</code>, <code>UsePointStyle</code> and <code>MultiKeyBackground</code>.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_13_Tooltip_Options)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Layout padding" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="layout-padding">
    <Block>
        Layout padding adds space between the canvas edge and the chart, legend and title. Chart.js uses no padding by default; the dashed border in the demo shows the canvas edge.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Layout.Padding = 20</code> to add 20 pixels on every side.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Layout.PaddingSides = new() { Left = 60, Top = 10 }</code> for a value per side; when set, it is sent instead of <code>Padding</code>.</li>
                <li>Leave both unset for no padding.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_14_Layout_Padding)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Aspect ratio and resize delay" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="aspect-ratio-and-resize-delay">
    <Block>
        A responsive chart keeps its width-to-height ratio while <code>MaintainAspectRatio</code> is <code>true</code>; Chart.js uses a ratio of 1 for radar charts. <code>ResizeDelay</code> waits the given milliseconds after the last resize before redrawing, so dragging the window edge causes one redraw instead of many; resize the window to see it.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Keep <code>MaintainAspectRatio = true</code> (the default) and set <code>AspectRatio</code> to 1, 2 or 3.</li>
                <li>Set <code>ResizeDelay = 250</code> to redraw 250 ms after resizing stops.</li>
                <li>Set <code>DevicePixelRatio = 2</code> to draw the canvas at twice the resolution, for sharper exported images.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_15_Aspect_Ratio_And_Resize_Delay)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Hover and elements" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="hover-and-elements">
    <Block>
        <code>Elements</code> sets default styles for every point and line the chart draws, and <code>Hover</code> decides which points are highlighted under the pointer, separately from <code>Interaction</code>, which the tooltip keeps using.
        Radar datasets always send their own <code>Tension</code> and <code>BorderWidth</code>, so set those on the dataset; dataset values win over <code>Elements</code>.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Elements = new ChartElements { Point = new ChartElementsPoint { Radius = 5, PointStyle = "rectRot", HoverRadius = 9 } }</code>.</li>
                <li>Add <code>Line = new ChartElementsLine { BorderDash = [6, 4] }</code> to dash every radar outline.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Hover = new ChartHover { Mode = InteractionMode.Index, Intersect = false }</code> to highlight all points on the hovered spoke while the tooltip still needs a point under the pointer.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Events = ["click"]</code> to listen to clicks only: moving the pointer does nothing and <code>OnHover</code> does not fire; clicking a point still highlights it and opens its tooltip, and <code>OnClick</code> fires.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_16_Hover_And_Elements)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Colors plugin" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="colors-plugin">
    <Block>
        The Chart.js colors plugin gives datasets that set no colors a color from its built-in palette. Line, radar and scatter datasets in this library always send a default color, so on a radar chart the palette applies only with <code>ForceOverride = true</code>.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Colors = new() { Enabled = true }</code>; radar datasets without colors stay grey, because they send <code>"rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)"</code>.</li>
                <li>Add <code>ForceOverride = true</code> to color every dataset from the palette.</li>
                <li>With <code>ForceOverride = true</code>, the palette also replaces colors you set on the datasets; set <code>Enabled = false</code> to turn the plugin off.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_17_Colors_Plugin)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

